// Moving-platform testbed — the map tapes/platform_ride.tape runs against.
//
// Two ledges with a spiked pit between them, and one platform ferrying across.
// The pit is lethal on purpose: `expect no died` is then a real assertion that
// the ride worked, rather than a statement that falling in was survivable.
//
// A new file rather than an addition to testbed_platform.ron, which is frozen —
// its tapes encode its exact pixels. Treat this one as a fixture too.
//
// Legend: '#' solid  '=' one-way platform  '^' spikes  'P' spawn.  Moving
// platforms have no grid character: a character can say where something is but
// not where it goes, so they are authored as `Platform(...)` entries below.
//
// 24x12 tiles at 32px = 768x384 world pixels.
//
//   spawn        col 2, row 7      world (70, 222), feet on the ledge at y=256
//   left ledge   cols 0-4          surface y=256, x 0..160
//   right ledge  cols 19-23        surface y=256, x 608..768
//   gangways     col 5 and col 18, row 8 — one-way plank, surface y=256
//   pit          cols 5-18         spikes at y 336..352, x 160..608 — the
//                gangways hang over its two ends
//   platform     3 tiles solid, top y=256 — level with both ledges and both
//                gangways, so it is walked onto and off rather than jumped
//                between.
//                from (6, 8) = x 192, a tile clear of the left ledge
//                to   (15, 8) = x 480, right edge 576, a tile clear of the
//                right one.
//                72 px/s over 288px = 240 ticks a leg, 480 ticks a round trip.
//                phase 420 puts it back at the left gangway on tick 60, which
//                is after the player has walked to the edge and stopped — the
//                whole point being that the ride is boarded, not jumped onto.
//
// ## Why the ferry stops a tile short, and why a plank fills the gap
//
// It used to dock flush against the ledge at x=160, and that was a crush: walk
// off the ledge as it comes home and it shuts on you against the ledge face,
// with nowhere legal for the physics to put a 20px body. A platform is only
// ever safe against a wall if it is *always* flush with it or *never* closer
// than a body — see the clearance rule in `tests/levels.rs`.
//
// So the path stops a tile short at both ends, and the last 32px is a one-way
// plank. A plank is a floor without a wall: the walk-on ride survives, and
// there is nothing there for the ferry to pin the player against. Falling into
// that 32px slot is the pit doing its job, not a crush — 32px holds a 20px
// player with room to spare.
Level(
    tileset: "castle",
    grid: [
        "########################",
        "#......................#",
        "#......................#",
        "#......................#",
        "#......................#",
        "#......................#",
        "#......................#",
        "#.P....................#",
        "#####=............=#####",
        "#####..............#####",
        "#####^^^^^^^^^^^^^^#####",
        "########################",
    ],
    entities: [
        Platform(from: (6, 8), to: (15, 8), tiles: 3, speed: 72.0, phase: 420),
    ],
)
